73797607421875, -0.015625, 0.038909912109375, 0.01425933837890625, -0.00830078125, 0.041229248046875, -0.02227783203125, 0.018951416015625, 0.004817962646484375, 0.018402099609375, 0.0306243896484375, 0.01506805419921875, 0.03662109375, -0.010406494140625, 0.0291900634765625, 0.0150299072265625, 0.01209259033203125, -0.00997161865234375, -0.0012559890747070312, -0.0102996826171875, -0.0308074951171875, 0.00872039794921875, 0.003078460693359375, -0.0092315673828125, 0.01044464111328125, 0.055145263671875, -0.033935546875, -0.0091094970703125, -0.0083160400390625, 0.00897216796875, -0.011749267578125, -0.004535675048828125, 0.0266571044921875, 0.0238494873046875, -0.0041351318359375, -0.01471710205078125, -0.023406982421875, -0.0169525146484375, 0.0032787322998046875, -0.031890869140625, 0.004108428955078125, -0.039886474609375, -0.01187896728515625, -0.006267547607421875, -0.01332855224609375, 0.01323699951171875, 0.0127410888671875, 0.006649017333984375, -0.02362060546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. In this context:\n\n- **Capability:** An operationally defined generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text prompts, measured by FID and CLIP score), image editing (modifying images based on instructions, measured by human-rated editing precision), or associating images with external knowledge (linking images to structured entities, measured by entity retrieval accuracy).\n- **Capability dependencies:** Empirically established relationships indicating that mastering one capability (e.g., text-to-image) facilitates learning another (e.g., image editing), determined via transfer experiments (see below).\n- **Capability milestones:** Predefined, quantitative performance thresholds (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) on held-out validation sets that indicate when a model has sufficiently acquired a capability to progress to more advanced tasks.\n- **Caption experts:** Ensembles of automated and human-in-the-loop systems that generate and harmonize image descriptions and entity tags across tasks, ensuring consistency and semantic alignment. Automated systems use LLMs and entity recognition models; human annotators review ambiguous or high-value cases.\n- **Harmonization module:** A dedicated system component that standardizes and aligns captions and entity tags across different data sources and tasks. For example, it resolves synonymy (e.g., \"dog\" vs. \"canine\"), enforces consistent entity linking, and unifies annotation formats, producing a single, coherent dataset for training.\n- **Curriculum scheduling:** Sequencing training data so that the model learns foundational capabilities before progressing to more complex ones, based on empirically determined dependencies and real-time model progress.\n\n**Workflow Overview**\n\nThe workflow proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **Data engines** generate and curate supervision for each capability:\n   - **Grounding Engine:** Constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation using automated web-scale scraping, filtering, and caption generation with large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for descriptive accuracy.\n   - **Transformation Engine:** Creates inter-image editing pairs by applying controlled transformations (e.g., object insertion, removal, style transfer) using automated image editing pipelines, labeling before/after pairs with specific edit descriptions.\n   - **Association Engine:** Links images to structured knowledge entities by matching image content to entries in external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata) using automated entity recognition models.\n\n2. **Caption experts** harmonize descriptions and entity tags across all data, ensuring consistency and facilitating cross-task learning. Human annotators review high-value or ambiguous cases.\n\n3. The **harmonization module** integrates these outputs, standardizing terminology and annotation formats to produce a unified dataset. For example, it maps synonymous captions to canonical forms and ensures entity tags are consistently linked to knowledge base identifiers.\n\n4. The **curriculum scheduler** sequences data exposure based on capability dependencies and model progress, dynamically adjusting the mix of data as the model reaches capability milestones. The scheduler evaluates progress every 10,000 training steps, with thresholds and adjustment rates (e.g., 20% sampling rate increase) determined via grid search on validation performance.\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Capability Dependencies**\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on empirically established capability dependencies. To determine these dependencies, we conduct transfer experiments as follows:\n\n- **Experimental Design:** For each pair of capabilities, we pretrain small models (n=3 per experiment, each with 500k parameters) on one capability (e.g., T2I) and then fine-tune on another (e.g., editing) using a fixed dataset of 100k samples per capability. Tasks and datasets are selected to represent the core generative skills targeted by the infrastructure, with samples drawn from the same distribution as the main training data to ensure relevance. Small models are used for efficiency and rapid iteration; key dependencies are validated at larger scale (see ablation studies below).\n- **Generalizability:** While initial transfer experiments use small models, we confirm that observed dependencies (e.g., T2I pretraining improves editing) persist in larger models (see ablation results). This informs the main training pipeline by dictating the order and timing of data exposure.\n- **Statistical Significance:** Improvements in downstream metrics (e.g., editing precision, entity retrieval accuracy) are measured. A dependency is established if pretraining yields a statistically significant gain of >5% (p < 0.05, paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction). Effect sizes and confidence intervals are reported to ensure robustness, and the threshold is validated across multiple tasks and datasets to avoid spurious dependencies.\n\n**Dynamic Curriculum Scheduling Mechanism:**\n\n- The model is first exposed to data for foundational capabilities.\n- When capability milestones (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) are reached on held-out validation sets, the scheduler introduces data for more complex capabilities.\n- If evaluation reveals stagnation in a capability (e.g., FID or CLIP score does not improve for 3 consecutive validation checkpoints, each checkpoint every 10,000 steps), the scheduler increases the sampling rate of relevant data by 20% until progress resumes. These parameters are fixed based on validation set performance and not tuned on test data.\n- Task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution are progressively adjusted according to predefined thresholds and model performance.\n\n**Data Curation and Quality Assurance**\n\nData curation involves:\n\n- Automated filtering for image quality (BRISQUE < 30, aesthetic scores > 6.0), with thresholds based on prior work demonstrating strong correlation with human judgments of image quality (Mittal et al., 2012; Shu et al., 2021).\n- Deduplication (perceptual hashing with Hamming distance < 10) to ensure dataset diversity, following best practices from LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022).\n- Content safety (NSFW and watermark detectors with >95% confidence), referencing approaches from OpenAI\u2019s CLIP and LAION datasets.\n- Only images passing these filters are retained.\n\nRecaptioning and entity linking are performed by caption experts, with human review for samples that fail automated confidence thresholds (<0.85). Strict quality and diversity criteria include minimum caption length (\u22658 tokens, following Sharma et al., 2018), vocabulary coverage (at least 90% of a 10,000-word reference lexicon, as in LAION-5B), and entity diversity (minimum 500 unique entities per 100,000 images, based on coverage analysis in Conceptual Captions), ensuring broad representation across concepts.\n\n**Relation to Prior Work and Ablation Studies**\n\nOur approach builds on and extends prior work in data curation and curriculum learning for generative models. For example, LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and Conceptual Captions (Sharma et al., 2018) focus on large-scale, high-quality text-image pairs, but do not address cross-task harmonization or capability dependencies. Curriculum GANs (Karras et al., 2018) and staged training in DALL-E 2 (Ramesh et al., 2022) introduce curriculum learning for generative models, but use fixed-stage training and do not empirically model dependencies between generative skills.\n\n**Ablation Studies:** To isolate the impact of each component, we conduct ablations on the 6B-parameter model:\n- **No harmonization:** Removing the harmonization module reduces editing precision by 0.3 and entity retrieval accuracy by 4%.\n- **No curriculum scheduling:** Using a fixed-stage curriculum increases FID by 0.7 and reduces editing precision by 0.2.\n- **No capability dependency modeling:** Randomized curriculum order reduces knowledge association accuracy by 5%.\n\n**Quantitative Comparisons:** Our infrastructure:\n- Empirically establishes and leverages dependencies between generative skills through transfer experiments, resulting in improved downstream performance (e.g., editing precision increases by 7% when preceded by T2I pretraining).\n- Ensures consistent annotation across tasks via the harmonization module, whereas previous work often uses task-specific annotation schemes, leading to inconsistencies.\n- Adapts data exposure dynamically based on real-time model progress, rather than relying on fixed-stage training.\n- Supports and is evaluated on a wider range of generative tasks (text-to-image, editing, knowledge association) within a unified framework, as demonstrated by improved performance across all CPI-Bench tasks compared to baselines.\n\n**Evaluation**\n\n*Experimental Setup:* We train 6B-parameter models on datasets of 200M images, with 10% reserved for validation and a fixed random seed for reproducibility. Hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, optimizer settings) are provided in the supplementary material. Data splits and code for preprocessing and evaluation will be made publicly available.\n\n*Evaluation Metrics and Reporting:*\n- **Text-to-image:** Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID; lower is better) and CLIP score (higher is better).\n- **Editing:** Human-rated editing precision, defined as the average rater score (1\u20135 scale, where 1 = \"no visible edit,\" 5 = \"edit is precise and matches instruction exactly\").\n- **Knowledge association:** Entity retrieval accuracy (percentage of test images for which the correct entity is retrieved from a knowledge base).\n\n*Statistical Analysis:* For each metric, we use paired t-tests to compare our model to each baseline on the same set of prompts or images. The Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied to"}}